# <% .Provider %>

A [Mamela](https://github.com/ieyoukan/mamela) provider for the API `<% .API %>`, written with
provider/kit. Made by `mamela provider new`; start by replacing the example kind (a "thing") with
what your API manages.

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `<% .Pkg %>/<% .Pkg %>.go` | the provider: each kind's calls, how to list its objects, what a declaration changes |
| `<% .Pkg %>/fakeapi` | a fake of the API, for the tests |
| `<% .Pkg %>/<% .Pkg %>_test.go` | Mamela's conformance suite against the fake, and the check that the Pome is the build |
| `pome/catalogs/<% .CatalogFile %>` | the API's catalog: what each call does (read, create, change, lose-data) |
| `pome/` | the Pome a declaration imports: the program, the catalog, and the generated Emela |
| `build.sh` | builds the program into the Pome and generates `pome/src/lib.emel` |

```sh
./build.sh      # after each change of the provider
go test ./...   # the conformance suite (needs Mamela's toolchain: mamela toolchain install)
```

A declaration uses it by importing the Pome:

```emela
import Mamela
import <% .Module %>.{<% .ThingType %>}

fn infrastructure() -> Mamela.Graph {
  Mamela.empty() |> <% .Module %>.<% .ThingFn %>(<% .ThingType %>(name: "web", value: "v1", tags: []))
}
```

and `mamela.providers.toml` says where the API is and names the catalog:

```toml
[provider.<% .Provider %>]
api_url = "https://..."
catalog = "pome:<% .CatalogName %>"
```
